phase2.explanation:
No bugs to chase this time — two habits worth locking in before Unit 4.

First: [[warn:"never catch exceptions, just let them crash" is the wrong instinct]] — catching and handling gracefully, WHEN you can actually do something useful with the failure, is exactly the point of this whole unit. What's genuinely bad is an empty catch block that swallows the exception and pretends nothing happened:
try {
    riskyOperation();
} catch (Exception e) {
    // silently doing nothing here is the actual problem
}
[[key:log it, recover with a sensible default, or let it propagate — anything except silence]].

Second: [[key:"file I/O is always slow" undersells what buffering and NIO actually do]] — reading a megabyte in one call is dramatically faster than reading it one byte at a time, and most of what felt slow about early I/O examples was the naive unbuffered approach, not something inherent to file access itself.
